`UDPLoadAssignment` (`agent/internal/xds/proxy/egress.go:421-438`) rewrites exactly two fields and `proto.Clone`s everything else:

```go
la, _ := proto.Clone(src).(*endpointv3.ClusterLoadAssignment)
la.ClusterName = clusterName
for _, lle := range la.GetEndpoints() {
    for _, lb := range lle.GetLbEndpoints() {
        sa := lb.GetEndpoint().GetAddress().GetSocketAddress()
        if sa == nil { continue }
        sa.Protocol = corev3.SocketAddress_UDP
        sa.PortSpecifier = &corev3.SocketAddress_PortValue{PortValue: port}
    }
}
```

So `Metadata` (including the `envoy.lb` subset keys), `Locality`, `Priority` and `Endpoint.HealthCheckConfig` all survive untouched.

## Why that is wrong for the waypoint case

`src` is the service's shared bare-name load assignment. When a waypoint is in play, `ServiceLocalityLbEndpointFromRegistryEndpoint` (`egress.go:572`, address built at `:626-637`) does not emit the pod's own address — it emits the **node IP plus the east-west tunnel port** (`DefaultEastWestTunnelPort`, `proxy/edge.go:74`), and tags the endpoint with `waypoint` subset metadata so the HTTP path selects it.

`UDPLoadAssignment` then rewrites only the *port*, leaving that node IP in place. The resulting `udp:` endpoint is `<nodeIP>:<appUDPPort>` — a host that was never the backend. The datagram goes to the wrong machine, and because the UDP floor has no transport socket and no response path assertion, nothing notices.

The surviving `Metadata` is inert rather than harmful (the `udp:` cluster sets no `LbSubsetConfig`, `egress.go:389-411`), but a non-zero `Priority` carried across would also silently partition a single-endpoint cluster.

## Why it is not covered

`TestUDPLoadAssignment` (`agent/internal/xds/proxy/egress_test.go:487-546`) hand-builds `src` as a plain single endpoint with no metadata, no priority and no health status, so none of this is exercised. `test/envoy_validate`'s fixture has the same shape — `meshInboundLoadAssignment` (`builders.go:1319

