## Change

GPUI users can now manage MCP access, as GTK users already can (#3805).

- **Shared service:** the in-process MCP service moves from `apps/linux` into `bookie-app-services::mcp_service`. GTK's `services/mcp_service.rs` is now a re-export, so both frontends use the same token store, scopes and allowlists. There is no GPUI-side policy.
- **GPUI settings card, new MCP group:**
  - enable or disable the server;
  - issue a read-only or read-write token scoped to the active saved connection. The plaintext is shown once, with **Hide**, and is never logged;
  - list tokens and revoke them.
- **Connection provider:** `AppConnectionProvider` now keeps weak references to every registered `DatabaseService`. GPUI registers each live session's service when it attaches.
- **GTK revoke:** it is now async, awaited through `glib::spawn_future_local`, which is the pattern GTK's `issue_token_flow` already uses. A failed revoke still shows the "Token not revoked" alert.

This finishes the work preserved on `wip/ux/gpui-mcp-settings-3805`. **Review fixes on top of the WIP, which had never compiled:**
- `live.rs` named `DatabaseService` without importing it;
- token issue and revoke ran on GPUI's executor, but keyring and storage I/O needs tokio. They now go through `resolve_ui::blocking` on the background executor;
- the token buttons used hardcoded colours and no `debug_selector`, so the UI test could not find them. They now use the house `primary_button` and `ghost`;
- the new revoke test mutated a copy inside `async move`, so its assertion could never pass. The callback now records the id synchronously;
- `with_source` went over the function-size limit. The approval and host-key prompt setup is extracted into `install_prompts`;
- two doc comments in the moved file were removed (comment-line guard).

