> This issue was written with AI help. The reproductions were run against the versions below.

### What happened

After #2042, our test suite's element-properties readers pass 106 more cases and lose none; thank you. Our derived-property census, which counts per owner metaclass how many elements each property is filled on, also moved up almost everywhere. Four rows moved the other way: they matched the Pilot before #2042 and fall short of it now. Each reproduces in a few lines, with `bin/sysml-grpc` built at `aa03065f72` (before #2042) and at `2d8bbc7a96` (after), and the Python client of `2d8bbc7a96`.

**1. `Type::input` of a send action is empty.**

```sysml
package P {
    attribute def Sig;
    port def SP;
    part def T {
        port sp : SP;
        attribute gain;
        action sendRef send new Sig(gain) via sp;
    }
}
```

| Read | `aa03065f72` | `2d8bbc7a96` |
|--|--|--|
| `P::T::sendRef` `input` | two ReferenceUsages | **empty** |
| `parameter` | the same two, each with `direction` `in` | the same |

Census: SendActionUsage `input` filled on 13 of 13 (matrix models) and 23 (Pilot examples) before, 0 after. The Pilot fills 13 of 13 and 35 of 35.

**2. `Expression::function` of a constructor expression is refused.**

On the same model, for the constructor `new Sig(gain)`:

| Read | `aa03065f72` | `2d8bbc7a96` |
|--|--|--|
| `function` | `P::Sig` | unanswered: "Expression::function is not defined for a ConstructorExpression" |
| `behavior`, `type` | `Performances::Evaluation` | the same |

A ConstructorExpression is an Expression, so we expected `function` to be answered. Census: filled on 8 of 8 and 75 of 75 before, unanswered on all after; the Pilot fills 8 of 8 and 84 of 84.

**3. A transition with an `accept` trigger loses its target, and its source moves to the target state.**

```sysml
package Q {
    attribute def Sig;
    state def SD {
        entry; then a;
        state a;
            accept Sig
            then b;
        state b;
    }
}
```

### What happened, and what you expected instead

A reflective query that walks what a type or feature owns returns a Disjoining, FeatureInverting or TypeFeaturing whose elementId is blank when the relationship is written as a clause of the declaration:

| Declaration | Element with a blank `elementId` |
|---|---|
| `classifier X disjoint from A, B;` | each `Disjoining` |
| `feature k disjoint from f;` | the `Disjoining` |
| `feature g : B inverse of f;` | the `FeatureInverting` |
| `feature h featured by A;` | the `TypeFeaturing` |

Expected: each of these relationships has a stable, unique elementId, and it is the id of an element the converted graph contains, so a query result joins the graph.

## Cause

The two layers disagree about whether the element exists.

Grammar: KerML.xtext makes each clause an owned relationship of the declared element: 'disjoint' 'from' ownedRelationship += OwnedDisjoining, 'inverse' 'of' ownedRelationship += OwnedFeatureInverting, 'featured' 'by' ownedRelationship += OwnedTypeFeaturing.
Reflection (internal/semantic/semantics) follows the grammar and reports the relationship among the owner's ownedRelationship.
Conversion (internal/translate/export) writes the clause only as a collapsed property of the owner (sysml:disjointFrom, sysml:inverseOf, sysml:featuringType) and no relationship element. docs/reference/rdf-mapping.md documents this: the clause "stays a property" of its owner. Only the keyword-first form (disjoining D1 disjoint A from B;) is written as an element.
So queryexec.ElementIDOf is asked for the id of an element that was never written. This is the same cause that #2414 fixed for crosses, unions, intersects and differences.

### Reproduction

```shell
m.kerml:


package P {
	classifier A;
	classifier B;
	classifier X disjoint from A, B;
	feature f : A;
	feature g : B inverse of f;
	feature h featured by A;
	feature k disjoint from f;
