## What happens

The Symbol API reports the features of a conjugated port with the direction written on the original port definition, whichever port they are listed through.

```sysml
package A30 {
    private import ScalarValues::*;

    port def CmdPort {
        out attribute cmd : Integer;
        in attribute ack : Boolean;
    }
    part def Device {
        port pOut : CmdPort;
        port pIn : ~CmdPort;
    }
    part def SubDevice :> Device;
    part def Rack { part slot : Device; }

    part device : Device;
    part sub : SubDevice;
    part rack : Rack;
}
```

```python
import opensysml
model = opensysml.parse_file("A30.sysml")
p_in = model.get("A30::Device::pIn")
for member in p_in.members(inherited=True):
    print(member.name, member.metadata.get("direction"))
# cmd out      <- expected in
# ack in       <- expected out
```

The same answer comes back when `pIn` is reached through `A30::device`, through `A30::sub` (a usage of a specializing definition), through the chain `A30::rack` / `slot`, and when the members are listed through the conjugated port definition `A30::CmdPort::'~CmdPort'` itself.

## What is expected

`cmd` is `in` and `ack` is `out` as features of `pIn` and of `~CmdPort`; they stay `out` and `in` as features of `pOut` and of `CmdPort`.

- SysML v2 §7.13.3 (conjugated port definitions): a conjugated port definition has the features of its original port definition with `in` and `out` reversed, `inout` unchanged.
- SysML v2 §8.3.12.2 `ConjugatedPortDefinition` and §8.3.12.3 `ConjugatedPortTyping`: a port typed `~P` is typed by the conjugated port definition of `P`.
- KerML §8.3.3.1.3 `Conjugation` and §8.3.3.1.10 `Type`: the conjugated type inherits the features of the original type, "but with all input and output Features reversed"; `Type::directionOf(feature)` gives the direction of a feature relative to the type "taking conjugation into account", and `Type::input` / `Type::output` are defined by it.

