## Summary

`GetSymbol` takes one string and reads it as a qualified name first, then as a generated id. Two kinds of generated id are spelled so that a declared name can take the same spelling, so a declaration can shadow the element the id was generated for.

## Reproduction

Unnamed connector end (`<owner>__<connector>_pend<i>`):

```sysml
package Col__V__c_pend0 { part marker; }
package Col {
    port def P;
    part def V { port q : P; port r : P; connection c connect q to r; }
}
```

`GetSymbol("Col::V::c")` lists `Col__V__c_pend0` and `Col__V__c_pend1` in `childIds`. `GetSymbol("Col__V__c_pend0")` answers the package, not the end; `Col__V__c_pend1` answers the end. This is the documented behaviour today (`docs/reference/wire-contract.md`, conformance case `symbol_connector_end_shadowed`): a qualified name wins.

Positional id (`Owner::@N`): an unnamed member is listed as `Owner::@3`. `'@3'` is a legal unrestricted name (KerML 8.2.2.3), so a member declared `part '@3';` in the same owner has the qualified name the positional id is spelled as. Whether the two actually collide after quoting has not been checked and is part of this issue.

In the export the end case does not collide: the package's element id escapes its underscores (`Col_5f_5fV_5f_5fc_5fpend0`), the end's is `Col__V__c_pend0`. The ambiguity comes from `GetSymbol` accepting a qualified name and an element id in one field.

## Proposal

Give generated ids a spelling no qualified name can take, under these constraints:

- the Symbol API, `GetElementProperties` and the export keep reporting the same id for the same element, so clients can join the surfaces by id;
- every id a `childIds` or `members` entry reports resolves to the element it was reported for;
- a declaration is always reachable by its qualified name.

Options to weigh: a reserved sigil that cannot appear in an unquoted or quoted qualified name segment as the surfaces spell it; an explicit id-kind field on the request; or resolving elem

### Model and observed behaviour

```sysml
package Observatory {
    part def Pt;
    part def Holder {
        part 'two words' : Pt;
        part 'it\'s' : Pt;
    }
}
```

`sysml -convert ttl` writes the RDF `sysml:qualifiedName` of an element whose name is no basic name without the quotes of the unrestricted name:

| surface | observed | expected |
|---|---|---|
| `sysml -convert ttl`, `sysml:qualifiedName` | `Observatory::Holder::two words` | `Observatory::Holder::'two words'` |
| `sysml -convert ttl`, `sysml:qualifiedName` | `Observatory::Holder::it's` | `Observatory::Holder::'it\'s'` |
| `sysml -convert api-json`, `qualifiedName` (for comparison, correct) | `Observatory::Holder::'two words'` | same |

Only a name that would otherwise be misread as an address is quoted in the graph (`'x::y'`, `'@1'`, a name beginning with `'`), and then with the identity escaping of `naming.Segment`, not `escapedName()`.

### Why this is its own defect

Split out of #2214, which fixed the escape sequences the parser kept in the declared name. This one has a different root: the graph's `sysml:qualifiedName` is the mapping's own address of the element (`docs/reference/rdf-mapping.md` § element addresses; `internal/translate/export/address.go`, `naming.Segment`), the string the element IRI is encoded from and the key every reader of the graph resolves by (`addressOf`, `rdf_in.go`, `mapping_qualified_name.go`). The API-JSON writer derives the specification's spelling from it separately and keeps the address as `sysx:mappingQualifiedName`. Quoting the Turtle value therefore means separating the two in the graph as API-JSON already does — the address under a mapping predicate, `sysml:qualifiedName` as the derived property — and moving every reader, the element-IRI derivation's input, and the corpus round-trip baselines with it.

### What the specification requires

KerML 1.1 Beta 2 §8.3.2.1.2: `Element::qualifiedName` is built from `escapedName()` of the element and of each owning n

