## What and why

An end feature of a step did not implicitly redefine the end at the same position in the step's type, so the members of the redefined end were not visible in the step end's body:

```kerml
behavior Observe {
    step transfer : TransferBefore {
        end feature source { feature redefines sourceOutput; }   // error[unresolved]: sourceOutput
        end feature target;
    }
}
```

`Model.implicitEndRedefinitions` (`internal/semantic/semantics/connector.go`) only paired ends when the owner *and* the general type were connector-like (connector, association, interface, connection, flow, binding). The two gates are gone: every end feature with an owning type is paired, by position, against `endsOf` each direct general type, whatever the owner's kind. `effectiveEnds` and `declaredEndCount` likewise now collect inherited ends from every direct general, so a step typed by a behavior whose ends come from a general behavior (`behavior Derived specializes Base;`) pairs with them too. The other consumers of `ImplicitEndRedefinitions` (`masking.go`, `crossing.go`, `model.go` `directRedefinedFeatures`, `resolve/redefined_nesting.go`, `passes/w10b_cross_features.go`, `exec/runtime/subsetting.go`) carry no connector assumption of their own; they consume whatever the semantic edge returns.

