```sysml
attribute redefines target =

### Summary

When a requirement has an `assume constraint`, OpenSysML evaluates only the required constraints and ignores the assumptions. A subject for which the assumption is false is reported as violating the requirement.

The standard library defines the result of a requirement check as (`Requirements.sysml`, `RequirementConstraintCheck`):

```
return result = allTrue(assumptions()) implies allTrue(constraints())
```

so when an assumption is false the requirement holds.

### Reproduction

`assume.sysml`:

```sysml
package Repro {
    private import ScalarValues::*;
    part def Thing { attribute enabled : Boolean; attribute level : Integer; }
    requirement def LevelOk {
        subject t : Thing;
        assume constraint { t.enabled }
        require constraint { t.level == 1 }
    }
    // assumption false -> by RequirementConstraintCheck::result, LevelOk should hold
    part off : Thing { attribute :>> enabled = false; attribute :>> level = 5; }
    // assumption true, required constraint false -> LevelOk should fail
    part on : Thing { attribute :>> enabled = true; attribute :>> level = 5; }
    requirement levelOk : LevelOk;
    satisfy levelOk by off;
    satisfy levelOk by on;
}
```

```
$ sysml assume.sysml -satisfy
✓ package Repro
✗ satisfy levelOk by off fails (on Repro::off ID: 1)
  Required condition evaluated to false: t.level == 1
  standing: violated (witnessed: 1 run under reverse)
✗ satisfy levelOk by on fails (on Repro::on ID: 2)
  Required condition evaluated to false: t.level == 1
  standing: violated (witnessed: 1 run under reverse)
```

The REPL gives the same result:

```
%instantiate Repro::off
%requirement Repro::LevelOk off
✗ Requirement Repro::LevelOk failed (on Repro::off ID: 1)
  Required condition evaluated to false: t.level == 1
```

### Expected

- `satisfy levelOk by off` holds (assumption `t.enabled` is false).
- `satisfy levelOk by on` fails (assumption true, `t.level == 1` false).

### Version

Reproduced on linux-amd64 w

