## Summary

`AlphaStrikeUnit.setStructure()` assigns its value to `armor`, not `structure`.

`src/classes/alpha-strike-unit.ts` (upstream `master`, 9062f21c):

```ts
public setArmor( nv: number ) {
    this.armor = nv;
}
public setStructure( nv: number ) {
    this.armor = nv;   // should be this.structure
}
```

It came in with dc4d7899 ("Start of AS unit editor... refactoring and cleanup of internal AS Class", 2023-03-26).

## Reproduction

```ts
const u = new AlphaStrikeUnit();
u.armor = 4; u.structure = 3;
u.setStructure( 7 );
// expected { armor: 4, structure: 7 }
// actual   { armor: 7, structure: 3 }
```

This runs as a Vitest case against `master` and fails as shown.

## Impact

Nothing in `src` calls `setStructure()` (or `setArmor()`) today, so there is no user-visible effect yet. The
method is public and named for the AS unit editor, though, so any future caller would silently overwrite armor
and leave structure unchanged.

## Suggested fix

Change it to `this.structure = nv;` and add a regression test.

## Summary

`BattleMech.setEngine()` (`src/classes/battlemech.ts`) keeps the mech's previous engine when it is asked for a rating that no engine has. That includes rating **0**, which is how the code says "no engine": `reset()` calls `setEngine(0)`, and choosing **-Select Walking Speed-** (Walk MP 0) in the Mech Creator calls `setWalkSpeed(0)`, which calls `setEngine(tonnage × 0)`.

## Reproduce

**Mech Creator:** pick 50 tons and 4 Walking MP (engine 200, 8.5 t), then set Walking MP back to **-Select Walking Speed-**. The 200-rated engine stays and still counts 8.5 t toward the mech's weight.

**`reset()`:**

```ts
const mech = new BattleMech();
mech.setTonnage(80);
mech.setWalkSpeed(5);
mech.reset();
// 20 t, Walk 0 - but still a 100-rated, 3.5 t engine: 9.5 t used instead of 5 t,
// so the "reset" mech differs from `new BattleMech()`.
```

## Expected

No engine (`null`, the state a new `BattleMech` starts in) whenever no engine matches the requested rating, so Walk MP 0 and `reset()` leave no stale engine weight, cost or BV behind.

## Proposed fix

When the loop finds no matching rating, set `this._engine = null` before `_calc()`. Every engine consumer (weight, gyro, rating, cost, BV) already handles a null engine. PR to follow.
